// REJECTS: Unexpected token
//
// A MongoDB class mapping with `~mainCollection` written BEFORE `~filter`. The body below
// contains exactly the same three clauses as the one in ../fixtures/t2-mongodb.pure with the
// first two transposed.
//
// The ordering rule is PARTIAL, which is what makes it easy to get wrong: `~filter` is
// optional but must come FIRST if present, while `~mainCollection` and `~binding` are
// order-free relative to each other. Verified against this runner --
//     ~filter, ~binding, ~mainCollection   parses
//     ~mainCollection, ~binding            parses (filter omitted)
//     ~binding, ~filter, ~mainCollection   rejected
//     ~mainCollection, ~filter, ~binding   rejected (this fixture)
// -- so a rewrite cannot satisfy it either by fixing a total order or by accepting any order.
//
// Ordering constraints are the cheapest thing for a rewrite to lose: a loop over "whichever
// clause comes next" is simpler to write than a prefixed sequence, handles the legal corpus
// perfectly, and is silently more permissive. Nothing downstream cares -- the protocol object
// has three independent slots -- so the divergence is invisible until two parsers disagree
// about whether a file is legal.
//
// legend-engine has this same shape in several grammars; ./ordering-constraint-clauses.pure
// and ./ordering-view-modifiers.pure pin two others. This is the MongoDB one.
###Mapping
Mapping fixture::neg::MongoMappingOutOfOrder
(
  *fixture::neg::Person[person_id]: MongoDB
  {
    ~mainCollection [fixture::neg::PersonDb] Person
    ~filter [fixture::neg::PersonDb] activeOnly
    ~binding fixture::neg::PersonBinding
  }
)
